            <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
            
            
           Backtrack from <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dp[n][m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dp[n][m]</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="alg1.l14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{tgt}},\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{src}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>tgt</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mi>src</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{tgt}},\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{src}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
            
            
           <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{k}\leftarrow\mathcal{K}_{\text{tgt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{k}\leftarrow\mathcal{K}_{\text{tgt}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l15.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">{structure-preserving tokens}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
            
            
           <math id="alg1.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leftarrow\{0,\ldots,n{-}1\}\setminus\mathcal{K}_{\text{tgt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leftarrow\{0,\ldots,n{-}1\}\setminus\mathcal{K}_{\text{tgt}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l16.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">{edit-driving tokens}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{k},k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{k},k</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div></div></div>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Conditioning Refinement (CR)</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Sec.<a href="#S4" title="4 Motivation ‣ Conditioning Matters: Stabilizing Inversion and Attention in Diffusion Image Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, conditioning precision and structural alignment influence inversion stability and cross-branch attention coupling. To improve both properties, we refine the conditioning signals by expanding prompts with additional contextual attributes, structured descriptions and background information while preserving the shared semantic structure between the source and target prompts.
In practice, conditioning refinement can be instantiated using external language models to generate more descriptive prompts. However, our framework is not tied to any specific refinement model, and alternative strategies that improve conditioning precision while maintaining source–target alignment can also be adopted.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Token-wise Cross-Branch Attention Control (TCAC)</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While refined conditioning improves structural consistency, it also increases token complexity and may dilute edit semantics under attention normalization (as shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5 Method ‣ Conditioning Matters: Stabilizing Inversion and Attention in Diffusion Image Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). To mitigate this effect, we partition conditioning tokens into two groups using the Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) algorithm in Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5 Method ‣ Conditioning Matters: Stabilizing Inversion and Attention in Diffusion Image Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> between the tokenized sequences of refined source and target prompts: <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">structure-preserving tokens</span> <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{k}</annotation></semantics></math> shared across the two prompts, and <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">edit-driving tokens</span> <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> that appear only in the target prompt. Given this token partition, we apply asymmetric cross-branch attention control as demonstrated in Fig. <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5 Method ‣ Conditioning Matters: Stabilizing Inversion and Attention in Diffusion Image Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. During the cross-branch editing process, structure-preserving tokens follow standard feature injection from the source branch into the target branch to preserve background consistency, while edit-driving tokens <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> are reinforced by a amplification factor <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> during attention computation to compensate for normalization-induced dilution:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A[:,k_{i}]\leftarrow\lambda A[:,k_{i}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A[:,k_{i}]\leftarrow\lambda A[:,k_{i}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This asymmetric modulation preserves background structures while maintaining strong editing semantics under dense conditioning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
